{
  "_comment": "Annotated example for config/llm.json — copy the blocks you need into the real file. This file is never read by the app; it exists so the schema is discoverable without going to the source. JSON has no comments, so the notes live in _comment* keys, which the loader ignores. Resolution priority for the single-provider fields (highest first): CLI flag (e.g. --llm-provider) > env var (e.g. NORA_LLM_PROVIDER) > this file > built-in default.",

  "_comment_single_provider": "The single active provider — what every lane uses when no `providers` roster is configured. Leave a field empty to fall through to the next tier. llm_provider is one of: ollama | openai-compatible | mock. llm_base_url must include /v1 for openai-compatible endpoints.",
  "llm_provider": "",
  "llm_model": "",
  "llm_timeout": 0,
  "llm_base_url": "",
  "llm_api_key": "",

  "_comment_embeddings": "Embeddings are resolved separately from the chat LLM — a deployment commonly runs local embeddings against a remote chat endpoint.",
  "embedding_provider": "ollama",
  "embedding_model": "qwen3-embedding:4b-q8_0",
  "ollama_url": "",
  "ollama_timeout_s": 0,

  "_comment_stages": "Pipeline stages to skip on a re-run. Both default to false in code; this file is where a deployment pins them.",
  "skip_taxonomy": true,
  "skip_graph": true,

  "_comment_providers": "OPTIONAL named roster for the Ask page. Omit the key entirely (the normal case) and nothing changes: the controls are not rendered and the single-provider fields above are used exactly as before. Add entries and the Ask page gains a provider picker plus a Fast/Think toggle. A roster entry is INDEPENDENT of the Config-page DB — a named endpoint always means that endpoint, so the Config-page LLM fields do not affect Ask synthesis while a roster is configured.",

  "_comment_default_fallback": "Ids into `providers`. default_provider is what an asker who never touches the picker gets. fallback_provider is where a permanent refusal reroutes (the answer card names whichever endpoint actually answered, so a reroute is never silent). Omit both and the FIRST entry is the default, which is how rosters written before these keys existed behave. An id matching no entry is dropped with a warning rather than failing the app.",
  "default_provider": "dgx-130b",
  "fallback_provider": "internal-14b",

  "_comment_providers_fields": "id: stable slug, recorded on every answer as llm_provider_id. name: what the user sees — say what the infrastructure IS ('130B — DGX'), not its role ('primary'), because the role means nothing to the person asking. base_url: include /v1. model: the model tag on that endpoint. api_key_env: the NAME of an env var holding the key — never put a key in this committed file. supports_reasoning_control: see below. reasoning_control: HOW to say \"don't think\" on the wire for this endpoint — \"chat_template_kwargs\" sends {\"enable_thinking\": false}, \"reasoning_effort\" sends the OpenAI-standard reasoning_effort field, \"none\" says the endpoint honours neither. Omit it and a legacy roster keeps its old meaning (reasoning_effort when supports_reasoning_control is true). There is no default that works everywhere — see below. default_mode: fast | think, applied when the asker expresses no preference. timeout: per-request seconds for THIS endpoint; omit or 0 to use the built-in default (600) — a 130B endpoint and a small one rarely want the same ceiling.",
  "_comment_supports_reasoning_control": "Whether the ENDPOINT HONOURS THE KNOB — not whether the model reasons. These are independent: a thinking model with no exposed control is false, because nothing we send changes how much it thinks. Set it false and the toggle renders as 'Not supported' instead of offering a choice that would do nothing. It cannot be auto-detected: no OpenAI-compatible endpoint advertises the capability, and a probe only catches outright rejection — a server can accept reasoning_effort and silently ignore it. Verify with: python -m core.src.llm.llm_debug --complete --text 'ping'",
  "_comment_reasoning_control": "WHICH WIRE FORM the endpoint accepts, which is a separate question from whether it honours anything. The obvious assumption — that vLLM takes reasoning_effort and maps it to enable_thinking itself, so one form serves every OpenAI-compatible server — is FALSE, and was measured false on both endpoints of the internal roster: a real vLLM server rejected reasoning_effort outright (400 extra_forbidden, 'Extra inputs are not permitted'), and a non-vLLM OpenAI-compatible server accepted the field but rejected the value 'none' (400 literal_error; its enum is low|medium|high, and low still thinks). chat_template_kwargs {\"enable_thinking\": false} returned 200 and a thinking-free answer on BOTH. So the mechanism is declared per entry. Like supports_reasoning_control it is declared, never probed — for the same reason: a server can accept a field and silently ignore it.",
  "_comment_modes": "fast suppresses thinking using this entry's reasoning_control form. think sends NOTHING — no reasoning_effort, no chat_template_kwargs — so the model does whatever the deployment already configured; we never assert an effort level on its behalf. The Ask page shows exactly these two states; effort levels (low/medium/high) are deliberately NOT exposed there (D-216) and stay in golden_cli --reasoning.",
  "providers": [
    {
      "id": "dgx-130b",
      "name": "130B — DGX",
      "base_url": "https://dgx.internal.example/v1",
      "model": "qwen3-130b",
      "api_key_env": "NORA_LLM_DGX_KEY",
      "supports_reasoning_control": true,
      "reasoning_control": "chat_template_kwargs",
      "default_mode": "fast",
      "timeout": 900
    },
    {
      "id": "internal-14b",
      "name": "14B — internal",
      "base_url": "https://llm.internal.example/v1",
      "model": "internal-14b",
      "api_key_env": "NORA_LLM_API_KEY",
      "supports_reasoning_control": false,
      "reasoning_control": "none",
      "default_mode": "think"
    }
  ]
}
